>
  <p class="lead">
    Spain launched its digital nomad visa in 2023, making it easier than ever for remote workers
    to enjoy tapas, beaches, and siestas legally. Combined with the famous "Beckham Law" tax benefits,
    Spain offers one of Europe's most attractive packages for location-independent professionals.
  </p>

  <h2 id="introduction">Why Spain for Digital Nomads</h2>

  <p>
    Spain has long attracted travelers and expats, but the new digital nomad visa makes it
    particularly appealing for remote workers:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Climate:</strong> Over 300 sunny days in southern regions, mild winters</li>
    <li><strong>Lifestyle:</strong> Famous work-life balance, late dinners, vibrant social culture</li>
    <li><strong>Cost:</strong> Lower than Northern Europe, especially outside Barcelona and Madrid</li>
    <li><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> Good internet, modern coworking spaces, reliable transport</li>
    <li><strong>Tax benefits:</strong> Beckham Law offers flat 24% tax rate for qualifying nomads</li>
    <li><strong>Location:</strong> Gateway to Europe, Africa, and South America</li>
    <li><strong>Healthcare:</strong> Excellent public and private healthcare systems</li>
    <li><strong>Variety:</strong> Beach, mountains, cities, islands—all within one country</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="visa-overview">Spain Digital Nomad Visa Overview</h2>

  <p>
    Spain's "Ley de Startups" (Startup Law), enacted in December 2022, introduced the digital
    nomad visa as part of broader measures to attract international talent. The visa allows
    non-EU remote workers to live and work legally in Spain.
  </p>

  <h3>Key Features</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Initial visa for 1 year (some cases up to 3 years)</li>
    <li><strong>Renewals:</strong> Renewable for additional 2-year periods</li>
    <li><strong>Total stay:</strong> Up to 5 years, after which permanent residency is possible</li>
    <li><strong>Work flexibility:</strong> Up to 20% of income can come from Spanish clients</li>
    <li><strong>Family friendly:</strong> Dependents can be included in the application</li>
    <li><strong>Schengen access:</strong> Free travel within the Schengen Area</li>
    <li><strong>Tax benefits:</strong> Eligible for Beckham Law (24% flat tax rate)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="eligibility">Eligibility Requirements</h2>

  <h3>Income Requirements</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Individual:</strong> €2,334/month minimum (200% of minimum wage)</li>
    <li><strong>With spouse:</strong> Add ~€875/month</li>
    <li><strong>Per child:</strong> Add ~€292/month each</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Work Requirements</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Remote employee OR self-employed/freelancer</li>
    <li>At least 80% of income from non-Spanish sources</li>
    <li>Working relationship with employer/clients for at least 3 months prior</li>
    <li>For employees: company must exist for at least 1 year</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Other Requirements</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Non-EU/EEA citizen</li>
    <li>No criminal record</li>
    <li>Valid health insurance covering Spain</li>
    <li>Not have been legally resident in Spain in the past 5 years (for Beckham Law)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="required-documents">Required Documents</h2>

  <h3>Identity and Travel</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Valid passport (6+ months validity)</li>
    <li>Passport photos meeting Spanish requirements</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Employment Verification</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Employment contract or freelance contracts</li>
    <li>Letter from employer confirming remote work arrangement</li>
    <li>Proof company has existed for 1+ year (registration documents)</li>
    <li>Evidence of 3+ month working relationship</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Financial Documents</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Bank statements (3-6 months)</li>
    <li>Proof of income meeting minimum requirements</li>
    <li>Tax returns from home country</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Additional Requirements</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Criminal background check (apostilled)</li>
    <li>Health insurance valid in Spain</li>
    <li>Proof of qualifications/professional experience</li>
    <li>Completed application forms</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Note:</strong> Documents must be translated to Spanish by a sworn translator and
    apostilled. Requirements may vary by consulate—always confirm with your local Spanish
    consulate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="application-process">Application Process</h2>

  <h3>Option 1: Apply from Abroad (Standard)</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Gather Documents (2-4 weeks)</strong></p>
  <p>
    Collect all required documents, translations, and apostilles. This is often the most
    time-consuming part.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Apply at Spanish Consulate</strong></p>
  <p>
    Submit your application at the Spanish consulate in your home country. Book an appointment
    in advance—slots fill quickly.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Wait for Approval (20-30 days typically)</strong></p>
  <p>
    The consulate reviews your application. You may be asked for additional documents.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Travel to Spain</strong></p>
  <p>
    Once approved, enter Spain with your visa. You have 90 days to apply for your TIE
    (Tarjeta de Identidad de Extranjero—foreigner identity card).
  </p>

  <h3>Option 2: Apply from Within Spain</h3>

  <p>
    If you're already in Spain legally (tourist visa, etc.), you can apply at the UGE
    (Large Companies and Strategic Collectives Unit) in Madrid. This is often faster
    (around 20 days) but requires being physically present.
  </p>

  <h2 id="costs-timeline">Costs and Timeline</h2>

  <h3>Government Fees</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Visa fee:</strong> €80</li>
    <li><strong>TIE card:</strong> ~€16</li>
    <li><strong>Total official fees:</strong> ~€100</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Additional Costs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sworn translations:</strong> €50-200</li>
    <li><strong>Apostilles:</strong> €10-50 per document</li>
    <li><strong>Criminal record check:</strong> €20-50</li>
    <li><strong>Health insurance:</strong> €50-200/month</li>
    <li><strong>Immigration lawyer (optional):</strong> €500-2,000</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Timeline</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Document preparation:</strong> 2-4 weeks</li>
    <li><strong>Visa processing:</strong> 20-30 days</li>
    <li><strong>TIE appointment:</strong> 30-60 days after arrival</li>
    <li><strong>Total:</strong> 2-4 months from start to TIE</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="beckham-law">Beckham Law Tax Benefits</h2>

  <p>
    One of Spain's biggest draws is the "Beckham Law" (named after footballer David Beckham,
    who benefited from it). This special tax regime offers substantial savings for qualifying
    digital nomads.
  </p>

  <h3>How It Works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flat tax rate:</strong> 24% on Spanish income up to €600,000 (vs. progressive rates up to 47%)</li>
    <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Available for the year of arrival plus 5 following years</li>
    <li><strong>Foreign income:</strong> May be exempt from Spanish tax (with exceptions)</li>
    <li><strong>Wealth tax:</strong> Only on Spanish assets</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Eligibility for Beckham Law</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Not been a Spanish tax resident in the previous 5 years</li>
    <li>Moved to Spain due to an employment contract or as a digital nomad visa holder</li>
    <li>Must apply within 6 months of registering with Spanish Social Security</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important Considerations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Consult a Spanish tax advisor—rules are complex</li>
    <li>Consider implications for your home country taxes</li>
    <li>Beckham Law affects eligibility for some Spanish tax deductions</li>
    <li>Rules may change—verify current regulations</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="best-cities">Best Cities for Digital Nomads</h2>

  <h3>Barcelona</h3>
  <p>
    Spain's startup capital with a huge international community. Pros: beach, culture, excellent
    coworking, vibrant nightlife, international airport. Cons: most expensive Spanish city,
    tourist crowds, pickpocketing.
  </p>

  <h3>Madrid</h3>
  <p>
    Spain's capital, more Spanish than Barcelona. Pros: great food scene, central location,
    lower costs than Barcelona, excellent transport. Cons: hot summers, no beach, less
    international feel.
  </p>

  <h3>Valencia</h3>
  <p>
    The sweet spot between Barcelona and smaller cities. Pros: beach, affordable, growing
    tech scene, great weather, less touristy. Cons: smaller international community,
    fewer direct flights.
  </p>

  <h3>Canary Islands</h3>
  <p>
    Year-round spring weather on these Atlantic islands. Pros: ideal climate, lower costs,
    nature, surf scene. Cons: isolated, limited nightlife, smaller communities.
  </p>

  <h3>Seville</h3>
  <p>
    Authentic Andalusian charm. Pros: affordable, beautiful architecture, authentic Spanish
    culture, great food. Cons: extremely hot summers, smaller nomad community.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost-of-living">Cost of Living</h2>

  <h3>Barcelona Monthly Budget</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rent (1-bedroom):</strong> €900-1,500</li>
    <li><strong>Coworking:</strong> €150-300</li>
    <li><strong>Groceries:</strong> €200-300</li>
    <li><strong>Eating out:</strong> €200-400</li>
    <li><strong>Transport:</strong> €40-80</li>
    <li><strong>Utilities:</strong> €80-150</li>
    <li><strong>Health insurance:</strong> €50-150</li>
    <li><strong>Total:</strong> €1,620-2,880/month</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Valencia/Other Cities</h3>
  <p>
    Expect 25-40% lower costs than Barcelona, especially for rent. A comfortable budget in
    Valencia might be €1,200-1,800/month.
  </p>

  <h2 id="healthcare">Healthcare Considerations</h2>

  <h3>Initial Health Insurance</h3>
  <p>
    For your visa application, you need private health insurance with full coverage in Spain.
    Popular options include Sanitas, Adeslas, and international policies like SafetyWing or
    Cigna Global.
  </p>

  <h3>Public Healthcare</h3>
  <p>
    Once you're a legal resident and contributing to Social Security, you can access Spain's
    excellent public healthcare system. Many nomads maintain private insurance for faster
    access and English-speaking doctors.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2>Ready for Spain?</h2>

  <p>
    Spain's digital nomad visa, combined with the Beckham Law tax benefits, creates one of
    Europe's most attractive packages for remote workers. Whether you're drawn to Barcelona's
    beaches, Madrid's culture, or the Canary Islands' eternal spring, Spain offers something
    for every type of nomad.
  </p>

  <p>
    Start your application early, gather documents carefully, and consider professional help
    for tax optimization. Spain's bureaucracy can be slow, but the lifestyle rewards are
    worth the patience. ¡Buena suerte!
  </p>

  <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
</ArticleLayout>
